1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:04,350
The Bank That Spent a Century
Teaching Singapore to Save

2
00:00:04,350 --> 00:00:08,051
For a couple of generations
of Singaporean schoolchildren,

3
00:00:08,131 --> 00:00:11,180
the first bank account
was a small book filled a

4
00:00:11,260 --> 00:00:14,570
little at a time with
ten-cent stamps, bought at the

5
00:00:14,650 --> 00:00:18,300
school bookshop and pasted
into printed rows of squares.

6
00:00:18,300 --> 00:00:22,332
The stamps, the passbook, the
queue in the school hall on

7
00:00:22,412 --> 00:00:26,660
collection day all belonged to
the Post Office Savings Bank,

8
00:00:26,740 --> 00:00:30,556
an institution whose entire
purpose, from 1877 onward,

9
00:00:30,636 --> 00:00:35,325
was to get ordinary people into
the habit of putting money aside.

10
00:00:35,325 --> 00:00:39,918
The Post Office Savings Bank
opened on the 1st of January 1877,

11
00:00:39,998 --> 00:00:42,884
set up by the colonial government of the

12
00:00:42,964 --> 00:00:46,815
Straits Settlements —
Singapore, Penang and Malacca —

13
00:00:46,895 --> 00:00:50,900
and run across the counter
of the General Post Office.

14
00:00:50,900 --> 00:00:54,983
It was modelled on Britain's
own Post Office Savings Bank,

15
00:00:55,063 --> 00:01:00,008
created in 1861 on the theory that
a bank attached to the post office,

16
00:01:00,088 --> 00:01:04,171
with very low minimum deposits
and a branch in every town,

17
00:01:04,251 --> 00:01:09,850
could reach the small savers that
commercial banks had no interest in serving.

18
00:01:09,850 --> 00:01:13,831
Day-to-day operations sat
with the Postmaster-General;

19
00:01:13,911 --> 00:01:18,725
policy was set by a group of
trustees appointed by the Governor.

20
00:01:18,725 --> 00:01:24,102
In its first year the bank
had 211 depositors and

21
00:01:24,182 --> 00:01:29,750
$19,864.90 on deposit,
paying 5 per cent interest.

22
00:01:29,750 --> 00:01:33,437
By 1940 it held around 57,000

23
00:01:33,517 --> 00:01:37,025
accounts and $14.3 million.

24
00:01:37,025 --> 00:01:40,725
The Japanese occupation broke the thread.

25
00:01:40,725 --> 00:01:44,987
Depositors who had trusted their
savings to the bank found the

26
00:01:45,067 --> 00:01:49,550
money largely out of reach, and
the grievance outlasted the war.

27
00:01:49,550 --> 00:01:52,697
In August 1949 a letter
to The Straits Times,

28
00:01:52,777 --> 00:01:57,431
headed "A Raw Deal For The Small
Man," argued that the small saver

29
00:01:57,511 --> 00:02:01,375
who had "kept his mite in
the Post Office Savings Bank"

30
00:02:01,455 --> 00:02:05,400
through the occupation had
been left to carry the loss.

31
00:02:05,400 --> 00:02:09,625
The bank's shape changed in 1949 as well.

32
00:02:09,625 --> 00:02:14,356
On the 17th of December 1948 the
Federal Legislative Council in

33
00:02:14,436 --> 00:02:18,861
Kuala Lumpur passed the Post
Office Savings Bank Ordinance,

34
00:02:18,941 --> 00:02:24,359
described by the Attorney-General as
a measure to amalgamate the various

35
00:02:24,439 --> 00:02:29,250
post office savings banks of the
Federation of Malaya into one.

36
00:02:29,250 --> 00:02:34,200
Singapore, by then a separate Crown
Colony, was left with its own.

37
00:02:34,200 --> 00:02:38,186
The Morning Tribune recorded
on the 1st of January

38
00:02:38,266 --> 00:02:42,250
1949 that the ordinance
"comes into force today."

39
00:02:42,250 --> 00:02:46,963
From that point there were two
institutions with a common origin —

40
00:02:47,043 --> 00:02:50,158
the Federation of
Malaya's and Singapore's —

41
00:02:50,238 --> 00:02:56,620
linked for years by a two-way facility that
let a depositor withdraw in either territory,

42
00:02:56,700 --> 00:02:59,750
an arrangement that finally ended in 1973.

43
00:02:59,750 --> 00:03:05,120
Through the 1950s the
Singapore bank grew steadily,

44
00:03:05,200 --> 00:03:08,753
from about $27 million in deposits

45
00:03:08,833 --> 00:03:13,000
in 1949 to roughly $58 million by 1955.

46
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:14,850
Then it drifted.

47
00:03:14,850 --> 00:03:20,101
Between 1957 and 1966 deposits
fell back to around $37 million,

48
00:03:20,181 --> 00:03:23,824
as inflation ate into
the value of a savings

49
00:03:23,904 --> 00:03:27,801
account and commercial
banks began courting the

50
00:03:27,881 --> 00:03:32,450
customers the post office
bank had once had to itself.

51
00:03:32,450 --> 00:03:36,226
In 1966 the Finance
Minister, Goh Keng Swee,

52
00:03:36,306 --> 00:03:39,900
appointed a committee to review the bank.

53
00:03:39,900 --> 00:03:43,229
The changes that followed
were practical ones:

54
00:03:43,309 --> 00:03:48,936
withdrawal limits were raised from $200
every seven days to $500 every three,

55
00:03:49,016 --> 00:03:52,568
opening hours were extended,
interest on deposits

56
00:03:52,648 --> 00:03:56,718
was made tax-free, and the
bank began accepting passbook

57
00:03:56,798 --> 00:04:00,800
signatures in Chinese and
other non-romanised scripts.

58
00:04:00,800 --> 00:04:04,250
New accounts rose sharply the next year.

59
00:04:04,250 --> 00:04:07,988
In 1968 the government
instructed the bank to

60
00:04:08,068 --> 00:04:11,806
actively promote domestic
saving, and in 1969

61
00:04:11,886 --> 00:04:15,450
it took that instruction into the schools.

62
00:04:15,450 --> 00:04:18,987
The National School
Savings Campaign gave every

63
00:04:19,067 --> 00:04:22,300
pupil a card printed with rows of squares.

64
00:04:22,300 --> 00:04:26,230
Ten-cent stamps bought at
school were pasted in until the

65
00:04:26,310 --> 00:04:30,171
card was worth two dollars,
at which point it was handed

66
00:04:30,251 --> 00:04:34,050
in and credited to a Post
Office Savings Bank account.

67
00:04:34,050 --> 00:04:37,084
Within two years the campaign had reached

68
00:04:37,164 --> 00:04:40,050
full participation in primary schools.

69
00:04:40,050 --> 00:04:43,390
A relaunch in 1983 added the mascot most

70
00:04:43,470 --> 00:04:46,975
people now remember, Smiley the Squirrel.

71
00:04:46,975 --> 00:04:52,153
The institution itself was reconstituted
on the 1st of January 1972,

72
00:04:52,233 --> 00:04:56,252
when an Act of Parliament
turned it from a government

73
00:04:56,332 --> 00:05:02,361
department into a statutory board — first
under the Ministry of Communications,

74
00:05:02,441 --> 00:05:06,075
then, from 1974, under
the Ministry of Finance.

75
00:05:06,075 --> 00:05:09,625
Its first chairman was Tan Chok Kian.

76
00:05:09,625 --> 00:05:14,350
What the bank did with the deposits
was the point of the exercise.

77
00:05:14,350 --> 00:05:17,519
A savings bank of this
kind pools a very large

78
00:05:17,599 --> 00:05:21,262
number of small balances
and lends them, at low risk,

79
00:05:21,342 --> 00:05:24,450
to a single borrower —
here, the government.

80
00:05:24,450 --> 00:05:28,614
POSB money went into Singapore
Government securities,

81
00:05:28,694 --> 00:05:33,019
and from 1974, through a
subsidiary called Credit POSB,

82
00:05:33,099 --> 00:05:38,625
into housing loans for buyers of
Housing and Development Board flats.

83
00:05:38,625 --> 00:05:41,945
The bank built to teach
thrift had become one of

84
00:05:42,025 --> 00:05:45,425
the quiet sources of
finance for public housing.

85
00:05:45,425 --> 00:05:48,500
The deposit base grew accordingly.

86
00:05:48,500 --> 00:05:52,985
It passed one million
depositors and one billion

87
00:05:53,065 --> 00:05:57,725
dollars in 1976, and ten
billion dollars in 1986.

88
00:05:57,725 --> 00:06:00,992
Part of the reason the
bank could gather so much

89
00:06:01,072 --> 00:06:04,350
was that it was, physically,
almost everywhere.

90
00:06:04,350 --> 00:06:08,429
Because savings ran through the
post office, POSB had a counter

91
00:06:08,509 --> 00:06:11,994
wherever there was a post
office — in the town centre,

92
00:06:12,074 --> 00:06:16,220
in the older shophouse districts,
and, as public housing spread,

93
00:06:16,300 --> 00:06:20,525
in the void decks and neighbourhood
centres of every new estate.

94
00:06:20,525 --> 00:06:24,098
It opened its first
automated teller machine at

95
00:06:24,178 --> 00:06:27,595
its Newton branch in
1981 and, within a year,

96
00:06:27,675 --> 00:06:31,250
was announcing branches
run by machines alone.

97
00:06:31,250 --> 00:06:35,105
By the late 1990s it operated more than

98
00:06:35,185 --> 00:06:38,234
170 branches and over 950 ATMs,

99
00:06:38,314 --> 00:06:42,350
the largest such network in the country.

100
00:06:42,350 --> 00:06:46,325
The bank was renamed POSBank in 1990.

101
00:06:46,325 --> 00:06:49,500
Eight years later it was absorbed.

102
00:06:49,500 --> 00:06:54,461
On the 24th of July 1998 the
Finance Minister, Richard Hu,

103
00:06:54,541 --> 00:06:59,937
announced that DBS Bank would
acquire POSBank for $1.6 billion;

104
00:07:00,017 --> 00:07:04,450
the transfer was completed
on the 16th of November.

105
00:07:04,450 --> 00:07:09,794
At that point POSBank held
about $25.5 billion in deposits

106
00:07:09,874 --> 00:07:16,061
and $26.9 billion in assets, with
more than 3.3 million customers —

107
00:07:16,141 --> 00:07:19,975
in a country of about 3.9 million people.

108
00:07:19,975 --> 00:07:23,713
The government's stated reasoning
was that a combined bank

109
00:07:23,793 --> 00:07:27,268
would be better placed to
compete with full commercial

110
00:07:27,348 --> 00:07:31,284
banks at home and abroad, part
of a wider push to consolidate

111
00:07:31,364 --> 00:07:35,050
Singapore's local banks into
fewer, larger institutions.

112
00:07:35,050 --> 00:07:37,975
The POSB name was kept.

113
00:07:37,975 --> 00:07:42,559
DBS runs it as its retail and
heartland brand, still with the

114
00:07:42,639 --> 00:07:46,229
line it adopted in the
1990s, "Neighbours first,

115
00:07:46,309 --> 00:07:52,425
bankers second," and still with the largest
branch and ATM network in Singapore.

116
00:07:52,425 --> 00:07:56,571
The tax exemption on
POSB deposit interest,

117
00:07:56,651 --> 00:08:02,450
one of the 1966 reforms, was
phased out by the end of 2004.

118
00:08:02,450 --> 00:08:06,417
Where it fits in the bigger
story: the habit of saving that

119
00:08:06,497 --> 00:08:10,750
Singapore is now known for was
not purely a matter of culture.

120
00:08:10,750 --> 00:08:14,980
It was taught, deliberately
and at scale, by a 121-year-old

121
00:08:15,060 --> 00:08:19,144
institution that reached into
classrooms and void decks —

122
00:08:19,224 --> 00:08:23,235
and that most people under
forty have only ever known as

123
00:08:23,315 --> 00:08:27,625
four letters on a bank branch
that belongs to someone else.
